{"subjectId":"python","id":"concurrent-futures","title":"concurrent.futures","group":"Concurrency","order":59,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">One high-level API for both threads and processes: submit callables to an <em>executor</em> and get back <em>futures</em> — placeholders for results that will arrive later.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Future</strong> — an object representing a pending result. You can ask whether it's <code>done()</code>, block on <code>result()</code>, fetch its <code>exception()</code>, <code>cancel()</code> it, or attach callbacks.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-api\"></i>The Executor API</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ex.submit(f, *args, **kw)</code></td><td>Schedule one call → returns a <code>Future</code> immediately</td></tr><tr><td><code>ex.map(f, *iterables, timeout=None)</code></td><td>Like built-in <code>map</code>, run in parallel; results in <strong>input order</strong></td></tr><tr><td><code>ex.shutdown(wait=True, cancel_futures=False)</code></td><td>Stop accepting work; called automatically by <code>with</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>as_completed(futures, timeout)</code></td><td>Yield futures as they <strong>finish</strong></td></tr><tr><td><code>wait(futures, timeout, return_when)</code></td><td>Block until ALL_COMPLETED / FIRST_COMPLETED / FIRST_EXCEPTION</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-send\"></i>submit, result &amp; map</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\n\ndef square(x):\n    return x * x\n\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=3) as ex:\n    fut = ex.submit(square, 7)\n    print(fut.result())                   # 49  ← blocks until ready\n    print(fut.done())                     # True\n    print(list(ex.map(square, [3, 1, 2])))   # [9, 1, 4]  ← input order, always</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>as_completed: handle results as they arrive</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import time\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor, as_completed\n\ndef job(name, delay):\n    time.sleep(delay)                     # stands in for a network call\n    return name\n\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor() as ex:\n    futures = {ex.submit(job, n, d): n for n, d in [(\"slow\", 0.05), (\"fast\", 0.0), (\"mid\", 0.02)]}\n    finished = [f.result() for f in as_completed(futures)]   # completion order\nprint(sorted(finished))                   # ['fast', 'mid', 'slow']\nprint(len(futures))                       # 3</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Map each future back to its input with a dict (<code>{future: input}</code>), because <code>as_completed</code> yields them in whatever order they finish.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-octagon\"></i>Exceptions travel through the future</h3>\n<p>An exception raised in a worker is stored in the future and <strong>re-raised when you call <code>result()</code></strong> — nothing is printed if you never ask.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\n\ndef risky(x):\n    if x == 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"bad input 2\")\n    return x\n\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor() as ex:\n    futures = [ex.submit(risky, i) for i in range(4)]\n    outcomes = []\n    for f in futures:\n        try:\n            outcomes.append(f.result())\n        except ValueError as e:\n            outcomes.append(f\"error: {e}\")\nprint(outcomes)                           # [0, 1, 'error: bad input 2', 3]\nprint(type(futures[2].exception()).__name__)   # ValueError</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>With <code>ex.map</code>, the first failing item raises when the iterator reaches it, and you lose the remaining results. Use <code>submit</code> + per-future <code>try</code> when some inputs may fail.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock-pause\"></i>wait, cancel &amp; timeouts</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import threading\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor, wait, FIRST_COMPLETED\n\ngate = threading.Event()\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=2) as ex:\n    quick = ex.submit(lambda: \"quick\")\n    blocked = ex.submit(gate.wait)        # can't finish until the gate opens\n    done, pending = wait([quick, blocked], return_when=FIRST_COMPLETED)\n    print([f.result() for f in done], len(pending))   # ['quick'] 1\n    gate.set()</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import threading\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\n\nstarted, gate = threading.Event(), threading.Event()\n\ndef blocker():\n    started.set()\n    gate.wait()\n    return \"finished\"\n\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=1) as ex:\n    running = ex.submit(blocker)          # occupies the only worker\n    queued = ex.submit(print, \"never\")    # waits in the queue\n    started.wait()\n    print(queued.cancel(), queued.cancelled())   # True True  ← not started yet\n    print(running.cancel())               # False  ← already running: can't cancel\n    try:\n        running.result(timeout=0.01)\n    except TimeoutError:\n        print(\"timed out\")                # timed out\n    gate.set()\nprint(running.result())                   # finished</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>A timeout on <code>result()</code> only stops <em>you</em> waiting — the task keeps running. Threads can't be killed; design long tasks to check a stop <code>Event</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-phone-call\"></i>Callbacks</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\n\nlog = []\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor() as ex:\n    f = ex.submit(sum, [1, 2, 3])\n    f.add_done_callback(lambda fut: log.append(fut.result() * 10))\nprint(log)                                # [60]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-cpu\"></i>Processes &amp; interpreters: same API</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\n\ndef count_primes(limit):\n    return sum(all(n % d for d in range(2, int(n ** 0.5) + 1)) for n in range(2, limit))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    with ProcessPoolExecutor() as ex:     # one worker per CPU core by default\n        print(list(ex.map(count_primes, [10_000, 20_000], chunksize=1)))   # [1229, 2262]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Executor</th><th>Parallel CPU?</th><th>Default <code>max_workers</code></th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code></td><td>No (GIL)</td><td><code>min(32, cpu_count + 4)</code></td><td>I/O-bound; shares memory</td></tr><tr><td><code>ProcessPoolExecutor</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>number of CPUs</td><td>Arguments/results must pickle; needs <code>__main__</code> guard</td></tr><tr><td><code>InterpreterPoolExecutor</code> (3.14)</td><td>Yes</td><td>number of CPUs</td><td>Sub-interpreters in one process, each with its own GIL</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Size thread pools for the I/O you're waiting on (tens of workers is fine); size process pools to the number of cores.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Using a <code>ProcessPoolExecutor</code> for tiny tasks — pickling and IPC overhead will make it slower than a plain loop.</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What's the difference between ex.map and submit + as_completed?</summary><div><p><code>map</code> returns results in input order and raises the first exception when you reach it — simple and tidy. <code>submit</code> returns one <code>Future</code> per call; with <code>as_completed</code> you process each result as soon as it's ready and handle failures individually.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you get an exception out of a worker thread?</summary><div><p>Call <code>future.result()</code> — it re-raises the worker's exception in the caller — or inspect <code>future.exception()</code>. With raw <code>threading.Thread</code>, exceptions are only reported by <code>threading.excepthook</code>, which is why executors are usually nicer.</p></div></details></section>"}